!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math
!tags: Math Function Terminology

!id: lkwfZCmSSo
!front: How are the terms "function" and "mapping" related?
!back:
- A function \( f : A \to B \) assigns each input in \( A \) exactly one output in \( B \).
- A mapping is, in most elementary mathematics, the same kind of object as a function — the word is often chosen to emphasize movement from one set or space to another.
!related: Other common synonyms include map, transformation (common in linear algebra for linear maps), and operator (common when the input and output are themselves functions, e.g. differential operators).
~~~

!id: qCBjb6Wuzy
!front: What is the difference between the codomain and the range of a function \( f : A \to B \)?
!back:
- The codomain is the declared target set \( B \).
- The range, or image, is the set of outputs that actually occur:

\[ \operatorname{range}(f) = \{ f(a) : a \in A \}. \]

So the range is always a subset of the codomain.
~~~

!id: Hd7kO2DRdb
!front: For \( f : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R} \), what are its domain, codomain, and range?
!back:
- Domain: \( \mathbb{R}^2 \).
- Codomain: \( \mathbb{R} \).
- Range: Cannot be determined from the type signature alone; it depends on the specific formula for \( f \).
~~~

!id: Bcs8TlU21l
!front: For \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \) given by \( f(x) = x^2 \), what are its domain, codomain, and range?
!back:
- Domain: \( \mathbb{R} \).
- Codomain: \( \mathbb{R} \).
- Range: \( [0, \infty) \).
~~~

!id: tZvQWOU2fh
!front: What are the domain and range of \( f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-2} \)?
!back:
- Domain: \( \mathbb{R} \setminus \{2\} \), since \( f \) is undefined where the denominator vanishes.
- Range: \( \mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\} \), since \( \dfrac{1}{x-2} \) takes every nonzero real value but never \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: fXPFXHfN7C
!front: What are the domain and range of \( f(x) = \sqrt{x - 1} \)?
!back:
- Domain: \( [1, \infty) \), since the radicand \( x - 1 \) must be nonnegative.
- Range: \( [0, \infty) \), since the principal square root is nonnegative and attains every value in \( [0,\infty) \) as \( x \) ranges over the domain.
~~~

!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math (Staging)::Functions
!tags: Math Function

!id: 5IdgxuQIzt
!front: Define an injective (one-to-one) function \( f : A \to B \), and give the equivalent contrapositive form of the definition.
!back:
\( f \) is injective if for all \( x_1, x_2 \in A \):
\[ x_1 \ne x_2 \implies f(x_1) \ne f(x_2). \]
Equivalently, by contraposition:
\[ f(x_1) = f(x_2) \implies x_1 = x_2. \]
!extra: The contrapositive form is usually the more useful one in proofs: assume the outputs agree and derive that the inputs must agree.
~~~

!id: WQK7mhne1i
!front: Define a surjective (onto) function \( f : A \to B \).
!back:
\( f \) is surjective if every element of the codomain is attained:
\[ \forall\, b \in B,\ \exists\, a \in A \text{ such that } f(a) = b. \]
Equivalently, \( \operatorname{range}(f) = B \).
~~~

!id: i40wHtyH1W
!front: Define a bijective function \( f : A \to B \).
!back:
\( f \) is bijective if it is both injective and surjective. Equivalently:
\[ \forall\, b \in B,\ \exists! \, a \in A \text{ such that } f(a) = b, \]
i.e. every element of \( B \) has exactly one preimage in \( A \).
~~~

!id: QpbVHeA51V
!front: Compare injective, surjective, and bijective functions.
!back:
- Injective (one-to-one): distinct inputs always produce distinct outputs; no element of the codomain is hit more than once.
- Surjective (onto): every element of the codomain is hit at least once; the range equals the whole codomain.
- Bijective: both injective and surjective at once — every element of the codomain is hit exactly once, giving a one-to-one correspondence between domain and codomain.
~~~

!id: XPn6GGTji8
!front: Under what condition on \( f : A \to B \) does an inverse function \( f^{-1} : B \to A \) exist, satisfying \( f^{-1}(f(a)) = a \) for all \( a \in A \) and \( f(f^{-1}(b)) = b \) for all \( b \in B \)?
!back: Such an \( f^{-1} \) exists if and only if \( f \) is bijective.
~~~

!id: N03ykiZvrF
!front: What goes wrong when trying to define an inverse for a function \( f : A \to B \) that is injective but not surjective?
!back: Since \( f \) is not surjective, some \( b \in B \) has no preimage in \( A \), so \( f^{-1}(b) \) has nothing to map to. An inverse function can only be defined on the range \( f(A) \subsetneq B \), not on all of \( B \) — restricting the codomain to the range recovers a genuine inverse.
~~~

!id: gEdGdn3pX0
!front: What goes wrong when trying to define an inverse for a function \( f : A \to B \) that is surjective but not injective?
!back: Since \( f \) is not injective, some \( b \in B \) has more than one preimage in \( A \). Any candidate \( f^{-1}(b) \) would have to pick between them, so the inverse relation assigns more than one value to \( b \) and is not single-valued — hence not a function.
~~~

!id: baenNAap5K
!front: What is the standard strategy to prove that \( f : A \to B \) is injective, and what is the standard way to show it is not?
!back:
- To prove injectivity: take arbitrary \( x_1, x_2 \in A \), assume \( f(x_1) = f(x_2) \), and derive \( x_1 = x_2 \) algebraically.
- To disprove injectivity: it suffices to exhibit a single pair \( x_1 \ne x_2 \) with \( f(x_1) = f(x_2) \).
~~~

!id: FssRu3ImJ9
!front: What is the standard strategy to prove that \( f : A \to B \) is surjective, and what is the standard way to show it is not?
!back:
- To prove surjectivity: fix an arbitrary \( b \in B \), and solve \( f(a) = b \) for \( a \), exhibiting an explicit \( a \in A \) (or showing one must exist).
- To disprove surjectivity: it suffices to exhibit a single \( b \in B \) for which no \( a \in A \) satisfies \( f(a) = b \).
~~~

!id: h8hHpvfAfs
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of injectivity for a real function \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \).
!back: \( f \) is injective exactly when every horizontal line \( y = c \) intersects the graph of \( f \) at most once — this is the horizontal line test. It restates the definition geometrically: two points on the graph sharing a \( y \)-value would mean two distinct inputs sharing an output.
~~~

!id: FxoSL0gFtJ
!front: Why is it meaningless to ask whether a function is surjective without specifying its codomain?
!back: Surjectivity means the range equals the codomain, so the answer depends on which codomain is declared, not on the formula alone. For example, \( f(x) = x^2 \) is not surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \) (negative numbers are never hit), but the same formula is surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to [0, \infty) \).
!related: See the distinction between codomain and range above — surjectivity is exactly the claim that these two coincide.
~~~

!id: PaRLb8yPlY
!front: If \( g : A \to B \) and \( f : B \to C \) are both injective, is \( f \circ g : A \to C \) injective? Derive the result.
!back: Yes. Suppose \( (f \circ g)(x_1) = (f \circ g)(x_2) \), i.e. \( f(g(x_1)) = f(g(x_2)) \). Since \( f \) is injective, \( g(x_1) = g(x_2) \). Since \( g \) is injective, \( x_1 = x_2 \). Hence \( f \circ g \) is injective.
~~~

!id: 0b8AHb5wrS
!front: Construct counterexamples showing that both "\( f \) is injective" and "\( g \) is injective" are needed for \( f \circ g \) to be injective.
!back:
- If \( g \) is not injective: let \( g(x) = x^2 \) and \( f(x) = x \) (injective). Then \( (f \circ g)(x) = x^2 \), which is not injective.
- If \( f \) is not injective: let \( g(x) = x + 1 \) (injective) and \( f(x) = x^2 \). Then \( (f \circ g)(x) = (x+1)^2 \), which is not injective — e.g. \( x = 0 \) and \( x = -2 \) both give \( 1 \).
~~~

!id: R6Ry3Lwwcy
!front: If \( g : A \to B \) and \( f : B \to C \) are both surjective, is \( f \circ g : A \to C \) surjective? Derive the result.
!back: Yes. Let \( c \in C \). Since \( f \) is surjective, there is \( b \in B \) with \( f(b) = c \). Since \( g \) is surjective, there is \( a \in A \) with \( g(a) = b \). Then \( f(g(a)) = f(b) = c \). Hence \( f \circ g \) is surjective.
~~~

!id: R0YFCGbPdv
!front: Construct counterexamples showing that both "\( f \) is surjective" and "\( g \) is surjective" are needed for \( f \circ g \) to be surjective (as maps \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \)).
!back:
- If \( g \) is not surjective: let \( g(x) = x^2 \) (range \( [0,\infty) \)) and \( f(x) = x \) (surjective). Then \( (f \circ g)(x) = x^2 \) has range \( [0,\infty) \), not all of \( \mathbb{R} \).
- If \( f \) is not surjective: let \( g(x) = x \) (surjective) and \( f(x) = x^2 \) (not surjective). Then \( (f \circ g)(x) = x^2 \), again not surjective onto \( \mathbb{R} \).
~~~

!id: UkILFegOmy
!front: Is \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \), \( f(x) = x^2 \), injective? Is it surjective?
!back:
- Not injective: \( f(1) = f(-1) = 1 \), but \( 1 \ne -1 \).
- Not surjective: no real \( x \) satisfies \( x^2 = -1 \), so negative elements of the codomain are never hit.
~~~

!tags: Math Function Injective Surjective Bijective

!id: M6fdRkdMjH
!front: Is \( f : [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty) \), \( f(x) = x^2 \), injective? Surjective? Bijective?
!back:
- Injective: for \( x_1, x_2 \ge 0 \), \( x_1^2 = x_2^2 \implies x_1 = x_2 \), since both are nonnegative square roots of the same number.
- Surjective: for any \( y \ge 0 \), \( x = \sqrt{y} \ge 0 \) satisfies \( f(x) = y \).
- Bijective, since both hold. Restricting the domain to \( [0, \infty) \) is exactly what makes \( x^2 \) invertible, with inverse \( \sqrt{x} \).
~~~

!id: FMNVPxhHcH
!front: Is \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \), \( f(x) = x^3 \), injective? Surjective? Bijective?
!back:
- Injective: if \( x_1^3 = x_2^3 \), then \( (x_1 - x_2)(x_1^2 + x_1 x_2 + x_2^2) = 0 \). The quadratic factor equals \( \left(x_1 + \tfrac{x_2}{2}\right)^2 + \tfrac{3}{4}x_2^2 \ge 0 \), vanishing only when \( x_1 = x_2 = 0 \); in every other case it is strictly positive, forcing \( x_1 = x_2 \).
- Surjective: every real number has a real cube root.
- Bijective, since both hold.
~~~

!id: 6w9h5n4yMG
!front: Is \( f(x) = e^x \) injective and surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \)? What changes if the codomain is restricted to \( \mathbb{R} \to (0, \infty) \)?
!back:
- As \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \): injective, since \( e^x \) is strictly increasing so \( e^{x_1}=e^{x_2} \implies x_1=x_2 \); not surjective, since \( e^x > 0 \) for all \( x \), so no negative number or \( 0 \) is ever hit.
- As \( \mathbb{R} \to (0,\infty) \): bijective — injectivity is unchanged, and now every \( y > 0 \) is hit by \( x = \ln y \).
~~~

!id: ghGTKcye3m
!front: Is \( f(x) = \sin x \) injective and surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \)? What changes if it is restricted to \( [-\tfrac{\pi}{2}, \tfrac{\pi}{2}] \to [-1, 1] \)?
!back:
- As \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \): not injective, since \( \sin 0 = \sin \pi = 0 \); not surjective, since \( \operatorname{range}(\sin) = [-1,1] \ne \mathbb{R} \).
- Restricted to \( [-\tfrac{\pi}{2}, \tfrac{\pi}{2}] \to [-1,1] \): bijective — \( \sin \) is strictly increasing on this interval, and attains every value in \( [-1,1] \), with endpoints \( \sin(-\tfrac{\pi}{2}) = -1 \) and \( \sin(\tfrac{\pi}{2}) = 1 \).
!extra: This restriction is exactly what makes \( \arcsin \) well defined as the inverse of \( \sin \).
~~~

!id: 1Dv8PWDtqT
!front: Is \( f : \mathbb{R} \setminus \{2\} \to \mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\} \), \( f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-2} \), injective? Surjective?
!back:
- Injective: if \( \dfrac{1}{x_1 - 2} = \dfrac{1}{x_2 - 2} \), taking reciprocals (valid since both sides are nonzero) gives \( x_1 - 2 = x_2 - 2 \), so \( x_1 = x_2 \).
- Surjective: for any \( y \ne 0 \), \( x = 2 + \dfrac{1}{y} \) lies in the domain (\( x \ne 2 \) since \( \tfrac{1}{y} \ne 0 \)) and satisfies \( f(x) = y \).
- Hence \( f \) is bijective onto \( \mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\} \).
~~~

!tags: Math Function Even-Odd

!id: hFbhcohEa7
!front: Define an even function \( f \).
!back:
\( f \) is even if its domain is symmetric about \( 0 \) (i.e. \( x \) in the domain \( \implies -x \) in the domain) and
\[ f(-x) = f(x) \]
for every \( x \) in the domain.
!extra: Domain symmetry about \( 0 \) is required simply so that \( f(-x) \) is always defined whenever \( f(x) \) is.
~~~

!id: MBwCUkVh4J
!front: Define an odd function \( f \).
!back:
\( f \) is odd if its domain is symmetric about \( 0 \) and
\[ f(-x) = -f(x) \]
for every \( x \) in the domain.
~~~

!id: K552GKr3cx
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of an even function's graph and an odd function's graph.
!back:
- Even: the graph is symmetric about the \( y \)-axis — reflecting it across \( x=0 \) leaves it unchanged.
- Odd: the graph is symmetric about the origin — rotating it \( 180° \) about \( (0,0) \) leaves it unchanged.
~~~

!id: 37n0gEIF30
!front: What is the only function that is both even and odd on a domain symmetric about \( 0 \)?
!back: The zero function, \( f(x) = 0 \) for all \( x \). If \( f \) is both, then \( f(-x) = f(x) \) and \( f(-x) = -f(x) \), so \( f(x) = -f(x) \), giving \( 2f(x) = 0 \), i.e. \( f(x) = 0 \) for every \( x \) in the domain.
~~~

!id: sUY7A4ufGO
!front: Show that every function \( f \) defined on a domain symmetric about \( 0 \) can be written uniquely as the sum of an even function and an odd function.
!back:
Define
\[ f_e(x) = \frac{f(x)+f(-x)}{2}, \qquad f_o(x) = \frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{2}. \]
Then \( f = f_e + f_o \), and substituting \( -x \) shows \( f_e \) is even and \( f_o \) is odd. For uniqueness: any decomposition \( f = g+h \) with \( g \) even and \( h \) odd forces \( f(-x) = g(x) - h(x) \), so \( f(x)+f(-x) = 2g(x) \) and \( f(x)-f(-x) = 2h(x) \), which pins down \( g = f_e \) and \( h = f_o \).
~~~

!id: VhRIFfLZnG
!front: How does the sum of two even functions, or two odd functions, behave under parity? What about the sum of an even and an odd function?
!back:
- even + even = even
- odd + odd = odd
- even + odd is generally neither: e.g. \( x^2 \) (even) plus \( x \) (odd) gives \( x^2+x \), which is neither even nor odd.
~~~

!id: TTyDcQ5obc
!front: How does the product of two even functions, or two odd functions, behave under parity? What about the product of an even and an odd function?
!back:
- even \( \times \) even = even
- odd \( \times \) odd = even
- even \( \times \) odd = odd
!extra: For example, if \( f,g \) are odd, \( (fg)(-x) = f(-x)g(-x) = (-f(x))(-g(x)) = f(x)g(x) = (fg)(x) \), so the product is even.
~~~

!id: z7xqkaTLgm
!front: If \( f \) is even and differentiable, what parity does \( f' \) have? What if \( f \) is odd?
!back:
- If \( f \) is even, differentiating \( f(-x)=f(x) \) by the chain rule gives \( -f'(-x) = f'(x) \), i.e. \( f'(-x) = -f'(x) \): \( f' \) is odd.
- If \( f \) is odd, differentiating \( f(-x)=-f(x) \) gives \( -f'(-x) = -f'(x) \), i.e. \( f'(-x) = f'(x) \): \( f' \) is even.
~~~

!id: oLHRFmuznb
!front: How do the values of \( \int_{-a}^{a} f(x)\,dx \) simplify when \( f \) is even, and when \( f \) is odd?
!back:
- Even: \( \displaystyle \int_{-a}^{a} f(x)\,dx = 2\int_0^a f(x)\,dx \).
- Odd: \( \displaystyle \int_{-a}^{a} f(x)\,dx = 0 \).
!extra: Both follow from splitting the integral as \( \int_{-a}^0 + \int_0^a \) and substituting \( x \to -x \) in the first piece: for even \( f \) the two pieces are equal, and for odd \( f \) they cancel.
~~~

!id: oClTGLT9gs
!front: Is \( f(x) = \sin x \) even, odd, or neither?
!back: Odd: \( \sin(-x) = -\sin x \) for all \( x \), and the domain \( \mathbb{R} \) is symmetric about \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: 3bv1VdnhU1
!front: Is \( f(x) = \cos x \) even, odd, or neither?
!back: Even: \( \cos(-x) = \cos x \) for all \( x \), and the domain \( \mathbb{R} \) is symmetric about \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: wThPYgRUl4
!front: Is \( f(x) = e^x \) even, odd, or neither?
!back: Neither: \( e^{-x} \ne e^x \) (e.g. \( e^{-1} \ne e \)), and \( e^{-x} \ne -e^x \) (since \( e^{-x} > 0 \) always, while \( -e^x < 0 \) always).
!extra: Being neither is compatible with the even/odd decomposition above: \( e^x = \cosh x + \sinh x \), an even part plus an odd part.
~~~

!id: W3Tb8VObaW
!front: Is \( f(x) = x^2 + x \) even, odd, or neither?
!back: Neither: \( f(-x) = x^2 - x \), which equals neither \( f(x) = x^2+x \) nor \( -f(x) = -x^2-x \) (except at \( x=0 \)). This is the standard example showing that even + odd need not be even or odd.
~~~

!tags: Math Function Monotonic

!id: XeWvKY3NLK
!front: Define what it means for \( f \) to be increasing on an interval \( I \), distinguishing the strict and non-strict (weak) versions.
!back:
For all \( x_1, x_2 \in I \) with \( x_1 < x_2 \):
- \( f \) is strictly increasing on \( I \) if \( f(x_1) < f(x_2) \).
- \( f \) is non-decreasing (weakly increasing) on \( I \) if \( f(x_1) \le f(x_2) \).
!extra: Terminology varies by author — some texts use "increasing" to mean strictly increasing by default. Always check which convention a source is using.
~~~

!id: cEYAP8r0F9
!front: Define what it means for \( f \) to be decreasing on an interval \( I \), distinguishing the strict and non-strict (weak) versions.
!back:
For all \( x_1, x_2 \in I \) with \( x_1 < x_2 \):
- \( f \) is strictly decreasing on \( I \) if \( f(x_1) > f(x_2) \).
- \( f \) is non-increasing (weakly decreasing) on \( I \) if \( f(x_1) \ge f(x_2) \).
~~~

!id: qFE68KjRsE
!front: What does it mean for \( f \) to be monotonic on an interval \( I \)?
!back:
\( f \) is monotonic on \( I \) if it is

- entirely increasing on \( I \) or
- entirely decreasing on \( I \) (in either the strict or weak sense).

The same one of the two behaviors must hold throughout \( I \), without switching partway.
~~~

!id: Bo9bNIki2F
!front: For \( f \) differentiable on \( (a,b) \), how does \( f \) being non-decreasing on \( (a,b) \) relate to the sign of \( f' \)?
!back:
\( f \) is non-decreasing on \( (a,b) \) if and only if \( f'(x) \ge 0 \) for every \( x \in (a,b) \).
- (\( \Rightarrow \)) If \( f \) is non-decreasing, every difference quotient \( \frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \ge 0 \), so its limit \( f'(x) \ge 0 \).
- (\( \Leftarrow \)) If \( f' \ge 0 \) throughout, the Mean Value Theorem gives, for \( x_1 < x_2 \) in \( (a,b) \), \( f(x_2)-f(x_1) = f'(c)(x_2-x_1) \ge 0 \) for some \( c \in (x_1,x_2) \).
~~~

!id: qe3mpWajsy
!front: Is \( f'(x) > 0 \) throughout \( (a,b) \) necessary for \( f \) to be strictly increasing on \( (a,b) \)?
!back: No — it is sufficient (by the same Mean Value Theorem argument, with strict inequality) but not necessary. For \( f(x) = x + \sin x \), \( f'(x) = 1+\cos x \ge 0 \), vanishing only at the isolated points \( x = (2k+1)\pi \); since \( f' \) is never negative and is zero only at isolated points, \( f \) is still strictly increasing on all of \( \mathbb{R} \).
~~~

!id: pND8gQbjAy
!front: Why does strict monotonicity of \( f \) on an interval \( I \) imply that \( f \) is injective on \( I \)?
!back: For \( x_1 \ne x_2 \) in \( I \), say \( x_1 < x_2 \). Strict monotonicity gives either \( f(x_1) < f(x_2) \) (increasing case) or \( f(x_1) > f(x_2) \) (decreasing case); either way \( f(x_1) \ne f(x_2) \).
~~~

!id: GS9d2GNYwC
!front: Construct a counterexample showing that an injective function need not be monotonic.
!back: Let \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \) be \( f(x) = 1/x \) for \( x \ne 0 \) and \( f(0) = 0 \). This \( f \) is injective on all of \( \mathbb{R} \) (\( 1/x \) is injective on the nonzero reals, and \( 0 \) is hit only at \( x=0 \)), but it is not monotonic: \( f(-1) = -1 \), \( f(-0.5) = -2 \), \( f(1) = 1 \), so \( f \) decreases from \( x=-1 \) to \( x=-0.5 \) but increases from \( x=-0.5 \) to \( x=1 \).
!extra: This relies on the discontinuity of \( f \) at \( x=0 \). For a continuous function on an interval, injectivity is in fact equivalent to strict monotonicity — see the next note.
~~~

!id: 9Yhyx3GAe7
!front: For \( f \) continuous on an interval \( I \), how does injectivity of \( f \) on \( I \) relate to strict monotonicity?
!back: They are equivalent: \( f \) is injective on \( I \) if and only if \( f \) is strictly monotonic on \( I \). Strict monotonicity always implies injectivity, with no continuity needed; the converse — injective implies strictly monotonic — requires continuity on an interval, and can fail without it.
!related: The discontinuous counterexample above shows why continuity is essential for the converse direction.
~~~

!id: JHumbSx0ep
!front: Is \( f(x) = x^2 \) monotonic on \( \mathbb{R} \)? Is it monotonic on \( [0,\infty) \)?
!back:
- On \( \mathbb{R} \): not monotonic — strictly decreasing on \( (-\infty, 0] \) and strictly increasing on \( [0,\infty) \), so neither behavior holds throughout.
- On \( [0,\infty) \): strictly increasing throughout, hence monotonic.
~~~

!tags: Math Function Asymptote

!id: 3HvAcxaw5c
!front: Define a vertical asymptote of \( f \) at \( x=a \).
!back: The line \( x=a \) is a vertical asymptote of \( f \) if at least one of the one-sided limits \( \lim_{x\to a^-} f(x) \) or \( \lim_{x\to a^+} f(x) \) is \( +\infty \) or \( -\infty \).
!extra: ![Vertical asymptote x = 2 for f(x) = 1/(x-2)](img/vertical_asymptote.svg)
~~~

!id: zzYdjtgp1r
!front: Define a horizontal asymptote of \( f \).
!back: The line \( y=L \) is a horizontal asymptote of \( f \) if \( \lim_{x\to\infty} f(x) = L \) or \( \lim_{x\to-\infty} f(x) = L \).
!extra:
A function can have different horizontal asymptotes (or none at all) in the two directions.

![Horizontal asymptotes y = ±π/2 for f(x) = arctan(x)](img/horizontal_asymptote.svg)
~~~

!id: qB9L6UhrH4
!front: Define an oblique (slant) asymptote of \( f \).
!back:
The line \( y=mx+b \), with \( m \ne 0 \), is an oblique asymptote of \( f \) as \( x\to\infty \) if
\[ \lim_{x\to\infty} \big(f(x) - (mx+b)\big) = 0, \]
and similarly as \( x \to -\infty \).
!extra: ![Oblique asymptote y = x for f(x) = (x^2+1)/x](img/oblique_asymptote.svg)
~~~

!id: Sv1uQRdxot
!front: Compare vertical, horizontal, and oblique asymptotes.
!back:
- Vertical asymptote \( x=a \): \( f \) grows without bound (\( \to \pm\infty \)) as \( x \) approaches the finite point \( a \).
- Horizontal asymptote \( y=L \): \( f \) tends to a constant \( L \) as \( x \to \pm\infty \).
- Oblique asymptote \( y=mx+b \): \( f \) tends to a nonconstant linear function as \( x \to \pm\infty \); it generalizes a horizontal asymptote to a nonzero slope \( m \).
~~~

!id: aycBf4wdsI
!front: For a rational function \( f = P/Q \), when does a zero of \( Q \) give a vertical asymptote, and when does it give a removable discontinuity instead?
!back: Write \( f \) in lowest terms (cancel any common factors of \( P \) and \( Q \)). A zero \( a \) of the reduced denominator gives a vertical asymptote at \( x=a \). If instead \( a \) was a common zero of the original \( P \) and \( Q \) that cancels away, \( f \) has a removable discontinuity (a hole) at \( a \), not a vertical asymptote.
!related: \( f(x) = \dfrac{x^2-1}{x-1} \) has a removable discontinuity at \( x=1 \), not a vertical asymptote — the factor \( x-1 \) cancels, leaving \( f(x) = x+1 \) for \( x \ne 1 \).
~~~

!id: YVFZJYht7X
!front: For a rational function \( f(x) = P(x)/Q(x) \), how does the horizontal asymptote (as \( x\to\pm\infty \)) depend on the degrees of \( P \) and \( Q \)?
!back:
- \( \deg P < \deg Q \): horizontal asymptote \( y=0 \).
- \( \deg P = \deg Q \): horizontal asymptote \( y = a/b \), the ratio of the leading coefficients of \( P \) and \( Q \).
- \( \deg P > \deg Q \): no horizontal asymptote.
!extra: Divide numerator and denominator by \( x^{\deg Q} \) to see each case: every term with a negative net power of \( x \) vanishes as \( x\to\pm\infty \), leaving only the ratio of leading terms (or a term that blows up, in the last case).
~~~

!id: 9AD7KhbUCr
!front: For a rational function \( f(x)=P(x)/Q(x) \), when does \( f \) have an oblique asymptote, and how do you find it?
!back:
Exactly when \( \deg P = \deg Q + 1 \). Polynomial division gives
\[ f(x) = (mx+b) + \frac{r(x)}{Q(x)}, \]
with \( \deg r < \deg Q \), so \( r(x)/Q(x) \to 0 \) as \( x\to\pm\infty \). The quotient \( mx+b \) is the oblique asymptote.
~~~

!id: EUyF0kd7hf
!front: Can the graph of \( f \) cross its own horizontal asymptote?
!back: Yes — a horizontal asymptote only describes end behavior as \( x\to\pm\infty \), not that the graph avoids the line elsewhere. For \( f(x) = \dfrac{\sin x}{x} \), \( \lim_{x\to\infty} f(x) = 0 \), so \( y=0 \) is a horizontal asymptote, yet \( f(x)=0 \) infinitely often, at every \( x=k\pi \) with \( k \ne 0 \).
~~~

!id: oqdDQ8l5Gm
!front: Can a function have different oblique asymptotes as \( x\to\infty \) and as \( x\to-\infty \)? Give an example.
!back:
Yes. For \( f(x) = \sqrt{x^2+1} \):
- As \( x\to\infty \): \( f(x) - x = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}+x} \to 0 \), so \( y=x \) is an asymptote.
- As \( x\to-\infty \): \( f(x)-(-x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}-x} \to 0 \), so \( y=-x \) is an asymptote.
~~~

!id: xWvUGS6lr7
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x-2} \).
!back: Vertical asymptote \( x=2 \) (denominator zero, no cancellation); horizontal asymptote \( y=0 \) (numerator has lower degree than the denominator). No oblique asymptote.
~~~

!id: hTjCFmOHhv
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{x^2+1}{x} \).
!back: Vertical asymptote \( x=0 \). Dividing gives \( f(x) = x + \dfrac{1}{x} \), and \( 1/x \to 0 \) as \( x\to\pm\infty \), so \( y=x \) is an oblique asymptote. No horizontal asymptote, since the numerator's degree exceeds the denominator's.
~~~

!id: uW5iBbeAze
!front: Find all asymptotes of \( f(x) = \dfrac{x^2-1}{x-1} \).
!back: None. The factor \( x-1 \) cancels: \( f(x) = x+1 \) for \( x\ne 1 \), so \( x=1 \) is a removable discontinuity (a hole), not a vertical asymptote. The simplified function is itself a line, so it has no horizontal or oblique asymptote either — it doesn't approach a different line at infinity, it is one.
~~~
